ontent":[{"type":"heading","text":"Overview","anchor":"overview","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A just intonation system is defined by a set of frequency ratios relative to the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"tonic, each derived from small-integer harmonics. Any number of tones per interval of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"equivalence is supported — from a simple pentatonic to Harry Partch’s 43-tone 11-limit scale."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"On initialization, ratios outside "},{"code":"[1, equivalenceRatio)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" are reduced by dividing by"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"equivalenceRatio","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" until they fall in range; exact multiples of "},{"code":"equivalenceRatio","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"(including 1:1) are silently discarded. The remaining ratios are deduplicated and sorted"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"ascending."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorTuning\/documentation\/IvorTuning\/JustIntonation\/ratios","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" property prepends the tonic (1:1) and appends the interval of equivalence,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"so it always spans one complete interval of equivalence.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Does not support standard pitch notation. Just intonation systems are too variable"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"to map reliably to the 35 pitch classes of standard notation: even 5-limit scales","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"contain enharmonic pairs that are distinct in just intonation but share a single","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"spelling, and higher-limit systems diverge fu